 in a certain context. We present a theory of institutional contextualism that focuses on the mechanisms by which actors adapt a policy design to fit a situation. We conceptualize institutions as phenomena that are constituted by a constant dialectic between text (the general blueprint) and context (the particular setting). The first half of this dialectic, which is the diffusion of the constitutive text or norm onto the institutional setting, has been discussed in the literature. Our research focuses on the second half, and we delineate, in concept, mechanisms for fitting the program to the local context. We then use a case study of improvised microfinance programs in Tamil Nadu, India, to illustrate how this occurs in reality. The research underscores the unexamined link between effective governance and contextual fit and offers a typology of mechanisms for fit that should inform future research.","oa":"hybrid","url":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11077-012-9163-9.pdf","pdf":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11077-012-9163-9.pdf","k":["Dialectic","Context (archaeology)","Typology","Contextualism","Sociology","Corporate governance","Epistemology","Political science"]},{"doi":"10.1007/s11077-012-9160-z","t":"Social process in grizzly bear management: lessons for collaborative governance and natural resource policy","a":["Lauren I. Richie","Joel Oppenheimer","Susan G. Clark"],"d":"2012-07-17","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11077-012-9160-z","pdf":"","k":["Stakeholder","Natural resource management","Corporate governance","Process (computing)","Stakeholder engagement","Natural resource","Collaborative governance","Resource (disambiguation)"]},{"doi":"10.1007/s11077-012-9159-5","t":"Who teaches and who learns? Policy learning through the C40 cities climate network","a":["Taedong Lee","Susan van de Meene"],"d":"2012-07-06","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11077-012-9159-5","pdf":"","k":["Social 